{
  "questions": [
    {
      "id": "q1",
      "text": "What is a nested loop?",
      "options": [
        "A loop that runs twice as fast",
        "A loop written inside the body of another loop",
        "Two loops written one after the other",
        "A loop that runs only once"
      ],
      "answer": 1
    },
    {
      "id": "q2",
      "text": "In the dice example, `for die1 in range(1, 7)` holds `for die2 in range(1, 7)`. How many lines does it print?",
      "options": ["6", "12", "36", "49"],
      "answer": 2
    },
    {
      "id": "q3",
      "text": "In the dice example, what does the inner loop do while die1 is 1?",
      "options": [
        "It runs all the way, die2 from 1 to 6",
        "It runs once, with die2 = 1",
        "It waits until die1 reaches 6",
        "It runs only when die1 equals die2"
      ],
      "answer": 0
    },
    {
      "id": "q4",
      "text": "In the lesson's clock picture, which loop is the minute hand?",
      "options": [
        "The outer loop, since it starts first",
        "Both loops, since they move together",
        "Neither, since a clock has no loops",
        "The inner loop, since it goes all the way round before the outer one moves a step"
      ],
      "answer": 3
    },
    {
      "id": "q5",
      "text": "What does this print?",
      "code": "for i in range(1, 3):\n    for j in range(1, 4):\n        print(f\"{i}{j}\")",
      "options": [
        "11\n21\n12\n22\n13\n23",
        "11\n12\n13\n21\n22\n23",
        "11\n12\n21\n22",
        "12\n13\n22\n23"
      ],
      "answer": 1
    },
    {
      "id": "q6",
      "text": "In the multiplication table, why does `print(\"---\")` show up after each row and not after each product?",
      "options": [
        "It sits in the outer loop but outside the inner one, so it runs once per row",
        "It is the last line of the program, so it runs last",
        "print() always waits for every loop to end",
        "Python prints lines of dashes after the numbers"
      ],
      "answer": 0
    },
    {
      "id": "q7",
      "text": "What does this print?",
      "code": "for i in range(2):\n    for j in range(2):\n        print(\"x\")\n    print(\"-\")",
      "options": ["x\nx\n-", "x\n-\nx\n-", "x\nx\n-\nx\nx\n-", "x\nx\nx\nx\n-"],
      "answer": 2
    },
    {
      "id": "q8",
      "text": "What does this print?",
      "code": "for i in range(2):\n    for j in range(2):\n        print(\"x\")\nprint(\"-\")",
      "options": ["x\nx\n-\nx\nx\n-", "x\n-\nx\n-\nx\n-\nx\n-", "x\nx\n-", "x\nx\nx\nx\n-"],
      "answer": 3
    },
    {
      "id": "q9",
      "text": "In the lesson's teams example, there are 2 teams and 3 players. How many lines does it print?",
      "options": ["5", "6", "3", "2"],
      "answer": 1
    },
    {
      "id": "q10",
      "text": "In the teams example, the outer loop goes through teams. Which line does it print first?",
      "options": [
        "Tommy plays for Sharks",
        "Tommy plays for Bears",
        "Sharks plays for Tommy",
        "Lance plays for Sharks"
      ],
      "answer": 0
    },
    {
      "id": "q11",
      "text": "`break` runs inside the inner loop. Where does the program go on?",
      "options": [
        "After both loops, at the end of the program",
        "At the first line of the inner loop, from the start",
        "After the inner loop: the outer loop goes on with its next value",
        "At the inner loop's next value, as with continue"
      ],
      "answer": 2
    },
    {
      "id": "q12",
      "text": "What does this print?",
      "code": "for i in range(1, 4):\n    for j in range(1, 3):\n        if i == 2:\n            break\n        print(f\"{i} {j}\")",
      "options": ["1 1\n1 2", "1 1\n1 2\n2 1\n3 1\n3 2", "1 1\n2 1\n3 1", "1 1\n1 2\n3 1\n3 2"],
      "answer": 3
    },
    {
      "id": "q13",
      "text": "What does `end=\"\"` change in `print(\"a\", end=\"\")`?",
      "options": [
        "The next print goes on the same line instead of a new one",
        "print shows nothing",
        "print adds an empty line after a",
        "print ends the program"
      ],
      "answer": 0
    },
    {
      "id": "q14",
      "text": "What does this print?",
      "code": "print(\"a\", end=\"\")\nprint(\"b\")\nprint(\"c\")",
      "options": ["abc", "ab\nc", "a\nb\nc", "a\nbc"],
      "answer": 1
    },
    {
      "id": "q15",
      "text": "What does an empty `print()` do after the inner loop that draws a row?",
      "options": [
        "It prints the word None",
        "It erases the row it drew",
        "It moves to a new line, so the next row starts on its own line",
        "It stops the outer loop"
      ],
      "answer": 2
    },
    {
      "id": "q16",
      "text": "What does this print?",
      "code": "for row in range(2):\n    for star in range(3):\n        print(\"*\", end=\"\")",
      "options": ["***\n***", "*\n*\n*\n*\n*\n*", "***", "******"],
      "answer": 3
    },
    {
      "id": "q17",
      "text": "In the Signal Tower mission, the user types 4. How many stars does the last row have?",
      "options": ["4", "5", "3", "10"],
      "answer": 0
    },
    {
      "id": "q18",
      "text": "In the Signal Tower mission, the user types 4. How many stars does the whole tower have?",
      "options": ["4", "10", "16", "8"],
      "answer": 1
    },
    {
      "id": "q19",
      "text": "In the Signal Tower solution, why does the outer loop use `range(1, rows + 1)` and not `range(rows)`?",
      "options": [
        "range(rows) would draw one row too many",
        "range can't start at 0 in a nested loop",
        "row then goes from 1 to rows, the number of stars each row needs",
        "So the tower comes out upside down"
      ],
      "answer": 2
    },
    {
      "id": "q20",
      "text": "In the Signal Tower solution, which part decides how many stars a row gets?",
      "options": [
        "The outer loop's range(1, rows + 1)",
        "The print() after the inner loop",
        "The input() line",
        "The inner loop's range(row)"
      ],
      "answer": 3
    },
    {
      "id": "q21",
      "text": "An outer loop runs 3 times, and each time the inner loop runs 5 times. How many times does the inner loop's body run?",
      "options": ["15", "8", "5", "3"],
      "answer": 0
    },
    {
      "id": "q22",
      "text": "What does this print?",
      "code": "for i in range(3):\n    print(i, end=\"\")\nprint()\nprint(\"done\")",
      "options": ["0\n1\n2\ndone", "012\ndone", "012done", "123\ndone"],
      "answer": 1
    },
    {
      "id": "q23",
      "text": "What does this print?",
      "code": "for row in range(1, 4):\n    for star in range(4 - row):\n        print(\"#\", end=\"\")\n    print()",
      "options": ["#\n##\n###", "###\n###\n###", "###\n##\n#", "##\n#"],
      "answer": 2
    },
    {
      "id": "q24",
      "text": "What does this print?",
      "code": "for word in [\"hi\", \"yo\"]:\n    for letter in word:\n        print(letter, end=\"\")\n    print(\"!\")",
      "options": ["h\ni\n!\ny\no\n!", "hiyo!", "hi\nyo\n!", "hi!\nyo!"],
      "answer": 3
    },
    {
      "id": "q25",
      "text": "A nested loop draws a rectangle of stars, row by row. Where does the empty `print()` go?",
      "options": [
        "In the outer loop, after the inner loop",
        "In the inner loop, after each star",
        "Before the outer loop",
        "After both loops, at the very end"
      ],
      "answer": 0
    },
    {
      "id": "q26",
      "text": "What does this print?",
      "code": "for i in range(1, 3):\n    for j in range(1, 4):\n        if j == 2:\n            continue\n        print(f\"{i}{j}\")",
      "options": ["11\n21", "11\n13\n21\n23", "11\n13", "11\n12\n13\n21\n22\n23"],
      "answer": 1
    },
    {
      "id": "q27",
      "text": "In a nested loop, whose variable changes more often?",
      "options": [
        "The outer loop's",
        "Both change at the same pace",
        "The inner loop's",
        "Neither, until both loops end"
      ],
      "answer": 2
    },
    {
      "id": "q28",
      "text": "What does this print?",
      "code": "for i in range(2):\n    for j in range(3):\n        print(j, end=\"\")\n    print()",
      "options": ["01\n01\n01", "000\n111", "012012", "012\n012"],
      "answer": 3
    },
    {
      "id": "q29",
      "text": "Can the inner loop use the outer loop's variable, as in `for star in range(row):`?",
      "options": [
        "Yes: it takes the variable's value at that pass, so each row can be different",
        "No: the inner loop can't see the outer loop's variable",
        "Only when the variable holds a list",
        "Yes, but only inside a function"
      ],
      "answer": 0
    },
    {
      "id": "q30",
      "text": "What happens when this runs?",
      "code": "for i in range(2):\nfor j in range(2):\n    print(i, j)",
      "options": [
        "It prints 0 0, 0 1, 1 0 and 1 1",
        "It stops with an IndentationError: the inner for has to be indented under the outer one",
        "It prints only 0 0",
        "It runs the inner loop once the outer one has ended"
      ],
      "answer": 1
    }
